  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-online-decision-protocol": [
      "## Definition\n\nFix a positive integer horizon $T$, a nonempty action set $K$, and, when choices or the environment are randomized, a probability space $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$. An online decision protocol has rounds $t=1,\\ldots,T$. Before round $t$, the learner has the history $h_{t-1}$ consisting of its earlier choices and all feedback and loss information revealed in earlier rounds. It chooses $x_t\\in K$ using only $h_{t-1}$ and any learner-private randomization available by that time. In a randomized protocol, $X_t$ is a measurable random element of $K$, meaning a measurable map from $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F)$ into $K$ equipped with its declared measurable structure. The environment supplies a real-valued loss function $f_t:K\\to\\mathbb R$; it may have fixed $f_t$ before the choice or choose it after the choice, as specified by the environment model, but the learner does not observe $f_t$ before choosing $x_t$. The learner incurs loss $f_t(x_t)$ and then receives feedback about round $t$ according to the feedback model. The history $h_t$ adds $x_t$, the feedback received, and any part of $f_t$ explicitly revealed in round $t$. No convexity, boundedness, or regret guarantee is part of this protocol definition."
    ],
    "def-adversary-and-feedback-model": [
      "## Definition\n\nIn an online decision protocol with horizon $T$, the environment and feedback model specifies how each loss function is chosen and what the learner observes after choosing $x_t$. **Full-information feedback** reveals the entire function $f_t$ (or, for $N$ experts, the full vector of their $N$ losses) after the round-$t$ choice. **Bandit feedback** reveals only the played loss $f_t(x_t)$ after that choice. An **oblivious adversary** fixes the whole sequence $(f_1,\\ldots,f_T)$ before the learner's random choices are realized; it may know the learner's rule but not its realized private randomization. A **non-anticipating adaptive adversary** chooses $f_t$ from the history available before $x_t$ is chosen, so $f_t$ may depend on earlier choices and feedback but not on the current choice or the learner's current private randomization. A **reactive adversary** may choose $f_t$ after observing $x_t$, so its round-$t$ loss may depend on that current choice. These adversary classes have different timing and are not interchangeable; a guarantee established for one class is not asserted for another. For the stochastic reward model, let $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R)$ be the smallest collection of subsets of $\\mathbb R$ that contains every open interval and is closed under complements and countable unions; its members are the Borel sets. A **stochastic finite-arm reward environment** has $N\\ge1$ arms, a fixed probability measure $P_i$ on $(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R))$ for each arm $i\\in[N]:=\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$, and finite first moments $\\int_{\\mathbb R}|y|\\,P_i(dy)<\\infty$ with means $\\mu_i:=\\int_{\\mathbb R} y\\,P_i(dy)$. At each round the learner chooses a measurable arm $A_t$ with values in $[N]$ equipped with its full power set, before the reward $Y_t$ is drawn. The fixed-law condition means that, for every $i$, every event $B$ determined by the history before the reward and the chosen arm with $B\\subseteq\\{A_t=i\\}$, and every $C\\in\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R)$, $\\mathbb P(B\\cap\\{Y_t\\in C\\})=\\mathbb P(B)P_i(C)$. The model also states explicitly the conditional-mean identity used below: $\\mathbb E[\\mathbf1_B Y_t]=\\mu_i\\mathbb P(B)$ for every such $B$. Here $Y_t$ is an integrable real random variable, $\\mathbf1_B$ is the indicator of event $B$, and the history-and-arm events are measurable in the common probability space. This stochastic reward model is distinct from adversarial loss models and supplies no regret guarantee by itself."
    ],
    "def-static-dynamic-and-strongly-adaptive-regret": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet a finite-horizon online protocol have horizon $T$, action set $K$, round losses $f_t:K\\to\\mathbb R$, and learner choices $x_t\\in K$. Whenever a minimum below is used, assume it is finite and attained. For a fixed comparator $u\\in K$, the realized regret is\n$$\nR_T(u):=\\sum_{t=1}^T f_t(x_t)-\\sum_{t=1}^T f_t(u).\n$$\nThe **static regret** is\n$$\nR_T^{\\rm stat}:=\\sum_{t=1}^T f_t(x_t)-\\min_{u\\in K}\\sum_{t=1}^T f_t(u).\n$$\nFor randomized learners or environments, **expected regret** is the expectation of the specified regret random variable over their joint randomness, when that random variable is measurable and integrable. In a stochastic finite-arm reward model with means $(\\mu_i)_{i\\in[N]}$, let $\\mu^*:=\\max_{i\\in[N]}\\mu_i$. The **pseudo-regret** is the random variable\n$$\n\\bar R_T:=\\sum_{t=1}^T(\\mu^*-\\mu_{A_t}),\n$$\nwhereas the **realized reward regret** is\n$$\nR_T^{\\rm rew}:=\\sum_{t=1}^T(\\mu^*-Y_t).\n$$\n\nFor dynamic comparators, suppose $K\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ for a positive integer $d$ and fix a norm $\\|\\cdot\\|:\\mathbb R^d\\to[0,\\infty)$, meaning $\\|v\\|=0$ exactly when $v=0$, $\\|\\lambda v\\|=|\\lambda|\\|v\\|$ for every $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$, and $\\|v+w\\|\\le\\|v\\|+\\|w\\|$. Given a comparator sequence $u_{1:T}=(u_1,\\ldots,u_T)\\in K^T$, the **dynamic regret** and its **path length** are\n$$\nR_T^{\\rm dyn}(u_{1:T}):=\\sum_{t=1}^T f_t(x_t)-\\sum_{t=1}^T f_t(u_t),\\qquad P(u_{1:T}):=1+\\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}\\|u_t-u_{t+1}\\|.\n$$\nFor a nonempty contiguous integer interval $I=[r,s]$ with $1\\le r\\le s\\le T$, its **interval regret** is\n$$\nR_I:=\\sum_{t=r}^s f_t(x_t)-\\min_{u\\in K}\\sum_{t=r}^s f_t(u),\n$$\nand the **adaptive regret** is $\\max_{1\\le r\\le s\\le T}R_{[r,s]}$. For an algorithm $A$ producing the choices $x_1,\\ldots,x_T$, write $R_I(A)$ for its interval regret on $I$. The algorithm is **strongly adaptive** relative to a specified loss, feedback, adversary, and comparator class only if there are a stated base algorithm $A_0$, constants $C>0$ and $p\\ge0$, and a specified bound $B_{A_0}(n)$ on $A_0$'s static regret at horizon $n$ in that same class, such that for every nonempty contiguous interval $I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,T\\}$,\n$$\nR_I(A)\\le C\\bigl(\\log(eT)\\bigr)^p B_{A_0}(|I|),\n$$\nwhere $\\log$ is the natural logarithm and $|I|=s-r+1$. Strong adaptivity is a regret property relative to intervals; it does not imply anytime statistical validity."
    ],
    "lem-finite-arm-regret-decomposes-into-expected-action-counts": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $T,N\\ge1$ be integers, let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a probability space, and let $A_t:\\Omega\\to[N]$ be measurable arm choices and $Y_t:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ integrable rewards for $t=1,\\ldots,T$. Let $\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_N\\in\\mathbb R$ be finite arm means and assume the conditional-mean identity\n$$\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[Y_t\\mathbf1_{\\{A_t=i\\}}\\right]=\\mu_i\\mathbb P(A_t=i)\\qquad(t=1,\\ldots,T,\\ i\\in[N]).\n$$\nSet $\\mu^*:=\\max_{i\\in[N]}\\mu_i$, define the pseudo-regret $\\bar R_T:=\\sum_{t=1}^T(\\mu^*-\\mu_{A_t})$, the realized reward regret $R_T^{\\rm rew}:=\\sum_{t=1}^T(\\mu^*-Y_t)$, and the number of pulls of arm $i$ by $N_i(T):=\\sum_{t=1}^T\\mathbf1_{\\{A_t=i\\}}$. Then\n$$\n\\mathbb E[R_T^{\\rm rew}]=\\mathbb E[\\bar R_T]=\\sum_{i=1}^N(\\mu^*-\\mu_i)\\,\\mathbb E[N_i(T)].\n$$"
    ],
